{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"disanalogies":["The stored heat release mechanism emphasizes heat capacity and material inertia, whereas this geometry mechanism emphasizes radiation exchange and boundary conditions","The anthropogenic heat thesis focuses on continuous night-time energy injection, whereas this mechanism assumes cooling of a finite daytime reservoir trapped by geometry","The evaporative cooling deficit mechanism focuses on latent heat partitioning and moisture availability, whereas this mechanism focuses on sensible heat retention via longwave radiation geometry"],"overturn_conditions":["Field measurements show no correlation between sky view factor and nocturnal cooling rates across different city morphologies","Sensitivity analysis demonstrates that thermal mass or anthropogenic heat flux explains more than 80% of the cross-city variance in the nocturnal UHI gap","Studies in low-SVF environments (e.g., dense forests) show cooling rates equivalent to high-SVF rural open fields"],"shared_structure":["Clear, calm night conditions remove solar input and mechanical mixing, making radiative flux the dominant driver of cooling","Surface and air temperatures diverge based on the differential rate of cooling between the urban and rural volumes","Daytime energy storage is a necessary precondition that supplies the heat to be slowly released overnight"],"source_memory_refs":["922de524bda89045091bffe27e5f684a241ceb548f72af2f4d986794af18a844","4537b03dbe868d2baff247f0b7d6046f845aba2e29dbff78317c8d8ef0a9dec1"],"transfer_claims":["If rural windbreaks or shelterbelts similarly reduce sky view factors, they should also exhibit reduced nocturnal cooling rates relative to exposed fields","Increasing building heights in a low-density city without changing anthropogenic heat output should still increase the nocturnal UHI gap by lowering the effective sky view factor","A city with high anthropogenic heat but very high sky view factor (flat, open sprawl) should have a smaller nocturnal UHI than a city with lower anthropogenic heat but deep street canyons"]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large city stays warmer after a clear, calm night mainly because its dense building geometry traps longwave radiation in street canyons, slowing nocturnal cooling; the cross-city size of this night-time gap scales most strongly with sky view factor, which varies with building height and built density.","counterconditions":["The effect would weaken substantially under overcast skies because clouds close the sky view, equalizing rural and urban longwave cooling","High wind speeds would mechanically mix the warm urban air with cooler rural air, erasing the calm-air radiation trap","A rural area with its own tall canopy (e.g., a dense forest) would show a similarly reduced sky view factor and smaller cooling rate, closing the gap","A city with extremely wide, low-density sprawl and unobstructed sky views would lose its nocturnal radiation trapping advantage"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"On a clear, calm night, rural terrain radiates heat freely to the cold sky and cools rapidly. In a city, the dense arrangement of tall buildings creates street canyons that block the view of the sky. This reduced sky view factor (SVF) continuously redirects and reabsorbs longwave radiation emitted by surfaces, drastically slowing the urban cooling rate. The nocturnal gap is therefore primarily a radiation-trapping effect, and its cross-city magnitude depends on urban morphology: cities with deeper, denser canyons have smaller SVFs, retaining more stored thermal mass and anthropogenic heat, which makes the heat island more pronounced.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","922de524bda89045091bffe27e5f6